bcr2rdacarrier  aIncludes bibliographical references and index  aThis book constructs input finite dimensional (FD) models that are amendable for numerical calculations and provides accurate representations for responses of dynamical systems to these inputs, i.e., numerical solutions of stochastic equations. It establishes conditions under which numerical solutions of these equations deliver accurate estimates of extreme responses of dynamical systems that are needed to, for example, predict extreme weather events and design reliable aircrafts. It is intended to serve a broad audience including graduate students, researchers, engineers, scientists and applied mathematicians interested in the formulation and solutions of complex stochastic problems. Includes algorithms for constructing FD models for a broad range of inputs and generating realizations of these models; Uses data-based estimates of extremes of outputs of stochastic equations based on high/low fidelity numerical solutions; Gives practical criteria for the convergence of numerical estimates of extreme system responses0 aIntroduction.-, Primer of probability theory -- , Random vectors and processes -- , Convergence of random elements -- , Extremes of random processes by finite dimensional (FD) models -- , Extremes of solutions of stochastic differential equations by finite dimensional (FD) models0 aOnline resource; title from PDF title page (SpringerLink, viewed February 5, 2025) 0aStochastic analysis.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh85128175 0aFinite element method.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh85048349 0aDynamics.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh85040316 6aAnalyse stochastique. 6aMéthode des éléments finis. 6aDynamique.40uhttps://link.springer.com/10.1007/978-3-031-75023-6xWMS  aAdded to collection springerlink.ebookscomplete  a92bSTFffi0019e9ae-25bf-4cb9-8c94-8c5da1ef7120s29cb385d-2c09-4b47-ae38-d75396083e8f04985cam a2200541 i in0000018820620250403134755.0m     o  d        cr un|---aucuu240920s2024    sz a    ob    000 0 eng d  a9783031651007q(electronic bk.)  a3031651006q(electronic bk.)  z97830316509947 a10.1007/978-3-031-65100-72doi  aspon1456603506  a(OCoLC-M)1456603506  aGW5XEbengerdaepncGW5XEdEBLCPdOCLCOdCStdUtOrBLW  aMAIN 4aHC79.E5 7aRNU2bicssc 7aSCI0260002bisacsh 7aRNU2thema04a333.7223/eng/202409201 aCampbell, Heather E.,d1961-eauthor.1https://id.oclc.org/worldcat/entity/E39PCjHtRY68xXY4mhrj8tt3Qq0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n201105080910aGreen gentrification and environmental injustice :ba complexity approach to policy /cHeather E. Campbell, Adam Eckerd, Yushim Kim 1aCham :bSpringer,c2024  a1 online resource (xii, 202 pages) :billustrations (some color)  atextbtxt2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier0 aChapter 1. Overview of the Issues and Introduction to Our Approach -- Chapter 2. The Environmental Injustice Problem and Its Links to Gentrification and Displacement: Literature Review -- Chapter 3. Complexity Approach to Urban Systems -- Chapter 4. Conceptual Clarification: Gentrification, and Displacement -- Chapter 5. New Amenities that Provide Jobs: Consequences of Density and Segregation -- Chapter 6. Clusters of Conditions for Green Gentrification -- Chapter 7. Simulating the Consequences of Policies that Improve Environmental Conditions -- Chapter 8. Mitigating Displacement from Green Gentrification: Examining the Role of Housing-Cost Control Measures -- Chapter 9. Distillations and Policy Recommendations  aThis book argues that, given the complex nature of the urban environment, we cannot find one optimal solution to reducing environmental injustice, in part because there is no singular cause. Environmental injustice emerges in particular settings because of the combined and interdependent effects of a variety of different policy and community characteristics. The authors argue that addressing these interlinked problems requires an understanding of the clusters of community and contextual factors that combine in a variety of ways to both create problems and imply policy approaches to managing them. They argue for the use of complexity-informed methods to assist in making public policy choices, such as Qualitative Comparative Analysis (QCA) and Agent-based Modeling (ABM), to enable us to better identify plausible solutions for specific contexts. This volume offers a new perspective for strategically managing urban policy that considers the risk of gentrification and gentrification-related displacement, with the ultimate goal of improving social justice. Environmental injustice, pollution remediation, gentrification, and displacement are interlinked problems, all of which impinge on social justice in US cities. However, public policy research, and often practice as well, has tended to separately consider urban policy issues such as environmental injustice, brownfields and other pollution remediation, how to redevelop neighborhoods, and how to contend with gentrification and displacement. In this book the authors take a new perspective to such intertwined urban policy issues, using complexity thinking and, more importantly, complex adaptive systems approaches, in order to develop context-sensitive policy approaches to managing these ongoing problems  aIncludes bibliographical references0 aOnline resource; title from PDF title page (SpringerLink, viewed September 20, 2024) 0aEnvironmental policy.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh85044197 0aEnvironmental justice.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh97002483 0aGentrification.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh85053952 6aEnvironnementxPolitique gouvernementale. 6aJustice environnementale. 6aEmbourgeoisement (Urbanisme) 7aenvironmental policy.2aat 7agentrification.2aat1 aEckerd, Adam,eauthor.1https://id.oclc.org/worldcat/entity/E39PCjtdGVMhjWJFyRCGGdpWMq0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n20150162751 aKim, Yushim,eauthor.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n201501627440zAvailable to Stanford-affiliated users.uhttps://link.springer.com/10.1007/978-3-031-65100-7xWMSySpringerLinkxProvider: SpringerxpurchasedxeLoaderURLxsp4xspon1456603506  aeLoaderRecord xsp4  a92bSTFffi001a5daa-64f1-4da9-a2e6-2ddf5a0a1543sa445dbfb-033b-40ad-95d9-9d40591b989c02387njm a22004817i in0000029853920250403134755.0m        h        sz zunnnnnuneucr nnannnuuuuu250212s2022    xx zznn o         n zxx d0 aJPG6722113010 aJPG6722113020 aJPG6722113030 aJPG6722113040 aJPG6722113050 aJPG67221130602aALCD-1213bALM Records  anmon1499158179  a(OCoLC)1499158179  aNAXOSbengerdaepncNAXOSdOCLCOdCstdUtOrBLW1 bd1784bd179600aQuintets for fortepiano and winds /cMozart, Beethoven 1a[Place of publication not identified] :bALM Records,c[2022]  a1 online resource (1 sound file)  a005331  aperformed musicbprm2rdacontent  acomputerbc2rdamedia  aonline resourcebcr2rdacarrier  adigital2rdatr  aaudio file2rdaft0 aMai Shigeoka, fortepiano ; Les Vents Romantiques Tokyo0 aPiano quintet in E-flat major, K. 452 / Mozart (25:52) -- Piano quintet in E-flat major, op. 16 / Ludwig van Beethoven (27:39) 0aQuintets (Piano, bassoon, clarinet, horn, oboe)0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/subjects/sh85110025 6aQuintettes (Piano, basson, clarinette, cor, hautbois)0(CaQQLa)201-00249921 aShigeoka, Mai,einstrumentalist.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n202402417812iContainer of (work):aMozart, Wolfgang Amadeus,d1756-1791.tQuintets,mpiano, oboe, clarinet, horn, bassoon,nK. 452,rE♭ major.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n8113365712iContainer of (work):aBeethoven, Ludwig van,d1770-1827.tQuintets,mpiano, oboe, clarinet, horn, bassoon,nop. 16,rE♭ major.0http://id.loc.gov/authorities/names/n810812402 aVents Romantiques Tokyo (Musical group),einstrumentalist.40zAvailable to Stanford-affiliated users.uhttp://stanford.naxosmusiclibrary.com/catalogue/item.asp?cid=ALCD-1213yNaxos Music LibraryxProvider: NaxosxCollection: Naxos Music LibraryxsubscribedxeLoaderURLxnm4xnmon1499158179  aeLoaderRecordxum4  aC0bNAXOSffi001a7818-a456-4e3b-b2d9-b2d2dced9a77s7b0f8645-4e67-45d1-bc82-2f16d8ad1dad